  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Breezy through sundown, then mostly clear and dry \nwith decreasing winds tonight through Saturday.\n\nIt's been a partly sunny day but also quite breezy with a mixing \ndepth to 800 mb, supporting westerly wind gusts in the 25 to as much \nas 35 mph this afternoon. Expect those conditions to continue \nthrough sundown, bringing a decrease in gust speeds as the mixed \nlayer decreases. The main issue for tonight is if winds can slacken \nenough to support patchy frost. A sprawling area of high pressure \ncentered over western NY/PA is forecast to move eastward overnight \ntonight but not really crest over Southern New England until \nSaturday morning. Efficient radiational cooling is expected tonight; \nthere may be just enough of wind (around 5 mph) to keep frost from \ndeveloping or in isolated coverage, and for those reasons haven't \nissued frost headlines for those non-Northwest MA zones where the \ngrowing season continues. If you do have crops or plants, it may not \nbe a bad idea to consider taking protective actions to prevent frost \ndamage, but I think the coverage ends up being too isolated. Lows \nforecast in the mid/upper 30s in the coolest outlying areas similar \nto surface dewpoints, and into the low to mid 40s for the urban \nareas and coastlines. \n  \nA chilly start to Saturday but high pressure becomes established \nacross Southern New England. Much of the day features full or mostly-\nfull sunshine and light northwest winds, but a canopy of high-level \nclouds/cirrus will be streaming in from the south and southwest. \nSeasonable highs in the mid 60s under full sun. High pressure then \nretreats eastward by Saturday night, supporting lows in the upper \n30s to the mid/upper 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next quick moving round of rain comes Sunday \nafternoon and evening, continuing overnight and exiting Monday \nmorning.\n\nThe latest guidance suite continued to exhibit large uncertain with \nthe timing of rainfall ending Monday morning. The deterministic GFS \nwas on the more progressive side, with many of the international \nmodels more closely aligned with a slower timing and closer track to \nsouthern New England. However, there is agreement on the overall \ntrend of drier conditions developing through the day. It is the \ntiming that will need to be refined with later forecasts. For now, \nheld on to a chance of showers towards the Cape and islands \nfor a time Monday morning. \n\nThe timing will also impact rainfall totals. At present, expecting \nthis will be a modest rainfall, with rainfall amounts generally \nbetween one half to one inch. A slower timing, should it occur, \nopens the door for rainfall up to 1.50\", mainly towards the Cape and \nislands.\n\nA brief dry period is anticipated for Monday afternoon and most of \nMonday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Quick round of rain around mid week, then dry \nweather returns by Thursday.  \n\nOur region should then get clipped by a pair of low pressures, each \ntaking a different track. The first should pass by to our north \nlater Monday night into Tuesday. Its cold front likely does not \nclear the coastal waters until late Tuesday night. This will be a \nperiod of more showery precipitation.\n\nAnother dry period is expected Wednesday though most of Thursday \nnight, with high pressure moving across northern New England.  \n\nThe forecast for next Friday is uncertain. A low pressure near James \nBay could swing its associated fronts across our region Friday into \nFriday Night. Have a mention in the forecast for more showers some \ntime Friday. However, this timing could be delayed with later \nforecasts.",
